Feature: Flight modes and handling
  The sim has three flight modes. M selects the next one: MANUAL, then [[ANGLE]], then
  [[ACRO]], then MANUAL again. L changes between high and low [[rates]]. X turns
  [[differential thrust]] on and off. The top line of the HUD shows all three, for example
  "ANGLE | high rates | diff thrust on". The sim starts in MANUAL, with high rates and
  differential thrust on.

  Expectation: ANGLE levels the plane for you
    In ANGLE, the sticks set the angle of bank and pitch. When you release the sticks, the
    plane levels its wings and nose. Use this mode when you learn to fly.
    Given the flight mode is MANUAL
    When the pilot changes flight mode
    Then the flight mode is ANGLE

  Expectation: In ANGLE, the stick sets the bank
    Hold A or D, and the plane banks to an angle of 55° or less. It does not roll over.
    Given the flight mode is ANGLE
    And the plane is flying
    When the pilot rolls the plane to the left
    Then the wings are banked

  Expectation: In ANGLE, the wings become level again
    Release the stick, and the plane levels its wings by itself.
    Given the flight mode is ANGLE
    And the plane is flying
    And the wings are banked
    When the pilot releases the roll stick
    Then the wings are level

  Expectation: ACRO holds the attitude
    In ACRO, the sticks set the rate of roll and pitch. When you centre the sticks, the plane
    holds its attitude, also when it is upside down. Use this mode for aerobatics.
    Given the flight mode is ANGLE
    When the pilot changes flight mode
    Then the flight mode is ACRO

  Expectation: MANUAL gives you the control surfaces
    In MANUAL, the sticks move the ailerons, the elevator and the rudder directly. Nothing
    helps you. The plane flies as the real plane does.
    Given the flight mode is ACRO
    When the pilot changes flight mode
    Then the flight mode is MANUAL

  Expectation: Low rates for gentle flying
    The control surfaces move less for the same movement of the stick, so the plane turns
    more gently. Use low rates for smooth flight and for a landing.
    Given the rates are high
    When the pilot changes rates
    Then the rates are low

  Expectation: High rates for tight turns
    The control surfaces move through their full range. Use high rates for tight turns and
    rolls.
    Given the rates are low
    When the pilot changes rates
    Then the rates are high

  Expectation: Differential thrust off
    Only the rudder turns the plane in yaw, as on a plane with one motor.
    Given differential thrust is on
    When the pilot toggles differential thrust
    Then differential thrust is off

  Expectation: Differential thrust on
    The motors help the rudder. The outer motor turns faster and the inner motor turns more
    slowly, so the plane turns more quickly in yaw.
    Given differential thrust is off
    When the pilot toggles differential thrust
    Then differential thrust is on
